{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 20, :comments ["The Gaon [Saadia] brought proofs from reason that it is impossible for 'fracture for fracture' to be taken literally, for the first came without intent and how is it possible to inflict an identical fracture, and if it is in a dangerous place he will die, and likewise with the eye, and one who has lost a third of his eye's light — how can one do so to the eye of the assailant? Therefore the tradition is correct, for the interpretation of all these is that there is upon him a ransom, and it is fitting to put out his eye if he does not redeem it. And if they raise a claim against us, should the assailant be poor, our reply is that the verse speaks of the majority case, and perhaps the poor man will become rich. This reply also applies to those who claim, should the assailant of the eye be blind: <b>so shall it done to him.</b> The bet is in place of 'upon,' as in 'upon which I ride' (asher anokhi rokhev bah), and there are many like this. Or its meaning is: so shall be done to him if he does not redeem it."], :book :leviticus, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 24, :notes {0 "I read 'asher anokhi rokhev bah' as Ibn Ezra's own shorthand for Genesis 31:38, supplying the citation he leaves implicit; the phrase literally means 'which I ride upon her.'"}, :source-witnesses [["On Your Way" "http://mobile.tora.ws/"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T19:12:16.228132648Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}